STM32F407
=========

A Cortex-M4 with 1M of flash, 128K of SRAM at 0x20000000 and 64K of
core coupled memory at 0x10000000, and a floating point extension that
is single precision only.  The same part family as the NUCLEO-F303RE
beside it, with four times the RAM and eight times the flash, and that
is the whole difference that matters: this board runs Limbo and that
one does not.

What was measured
-----------------

Under qemu, which models an STM32F405 as -M netduinoplus2 -- the same
die family, and the same CCM, SRAM and flash geometry:

	qemu-system-arm -M netduinoplus2 -nographic \
		-serial null -serial mon:stdio -kernel istm32f407

The two -serial arguments follow from CONSUSART.  qemu gives
serial_hd(0) to USART1, serial_hd(1) to USART2 and serial_hd(2) to
USART3, so a console on USART2 needs one -serial null before the real
one and a console on USART3 needs two.  Get it wrong and the console is
attached to a port the kernel never writes to, and the board looks
dead.

What that run does, typed at the prompt and answered:

	; ls /dis
	/dis/bind.dis
	/dis/cat.dis
	/dis/echo.dis
	/dis/lib
	/dis/ls.dis
	/dis/sh
	/dis/sh.dis
	; cat /dev/time
	         95           95356402154            1525862433              16000000
	; echo hello world
	hello world

and the FPREG, FPREG-S2 and TRAPREG checks the armv7m kernel prints at
boot all pass.

Where the memory went
---------------------

163840 bytes are left after the kernel.  Loading /dis/sh.dis asks
malloc for 73536 bytes for its decoded instruction array alone -- 6128
instructions at 12 bytes an Inst -- and on this board that is not an
optimisation question, it is the difference between having a shell and
not.  The mkfile beside this file sets XIPMODS=1, so utils/dis2s lays
every module's Inst array out at build time as a RODATA symbol in the
text segment, and the array costs flash instead of RAM.  The kernel
image is 537816 bytes of the megabyte.

Getting from 147456 bytes free to 163840 took four portable sizes that
were chosen for a machine with megabytes.  conf.base0 is PGROUND(end),
so the kernel's data and bss are charged to the pools in whole 4096-
byte pages, and each of these was measured from the linker's symbol
table:

	mntalloc.tagmask	8192	a bit for each of the 65536 tags
				9P can express (NMNTTAG, 1024 here)
	Uart.istage/ostage	4096	2048 each way in every Uart
				(STAGESIZE, 256 here)
	xlists.hole		2240	128 free-memory runs; this board
				has two banks (NHOLE, 16 here)
	kmesg			1536	the console log (KMESGSIZE)
	ilockpcs		1024	a debug ring nothing writes
				unless LOCKCYCLES is defined

Data and bss went from 48736 bytes to 32280, which is nine pages to
eight.  The last of those was worth a whole page on its own: at
KMESGSIZE 1024 the kernel ended 24 bytes into the ninth page.

What is not done
----------------

ls(1) runs on a freshly booted board and does not run after another
command has run first.  This is not a bug with a fix in this
directory.  The largest single thing the Dis heap is asked for is the
8192-byte buffer bufio(2) makes for each open file, and whether one
exists depends on where the previous command left its holes; at the
prompt main holds about 70K and the heap about 50K of the 163840, and
the failure is honest -- "out of memory: heap" is raised into the
shell, which prints it and carries on.  The pool percentages in the
configuration beside this file were chosen by measurement for that
behaviour and the comment there says what the alternatives did.

What ran on silicon
-------------------

It boots to a shell on the part.  Captured from the USB UART click in
mikroBUS 1, with the port held open across a reset -- which matters,
because the log is printed once in the first milliseconds and every
listen opened afterwards sees a board sitting silently at its prompt:

	Inferno/stm32f407
	14 holes free
	10000000 10010000 65536
	20008000 20020000 98304
	163840 bytes free
	poolsize max 81900 contig 0
	poolsize max 80262 contig 0
	poolsize max 1638 contig 1
	FPREG-OK all 32 single-precision registers round-tripped
	FPREG-S2-OK VLDMIA reaches S2
	procinit conf.nproc 5

	Inferno Fourth Edition (20151010)
	conf stm32f407 (1790040366) jit 0

	TRAPREG-OK R1 survives an exception
	Initial Dis: "/osinit.dis"
	armv7m init
	ARMV7M-INIT-OK
	;

The memory figures are the same on the part as under qemu, and the PLL
locks: apb1hz reads 42000000 and cpuhz 168000000, so the 168MHz from
the internal oscillator is real and the console divisor came from a
clock the kernel actually has.  Commands run.  ls(1) still fails on the
Dis heap, which is what the section below already said it would.

It was programmed with no probe and nothing soldered, over the USB HID
bootloader already in its flash, using the client in
tools/mikroe-uhb-patches.

Two faults only silicon found
-----------------------------

The console stopped after exactly 408 bytes, four into "Inferno Fourth
Edition", on every boot -- and the kernel carried on past it.  LD2
blinks from idlehands(), so it had reached the scheduler and was
idling: never hung, just talking into a queue nothing emptied.
putstrn0() writes straight to uartputs() only while serialoq is nil,
devuart.c points serialoq at the uart's output queue as soon as one
becomes the console, and stm32f4_kick() then broke out of its loop the
moment TXE was clear -- one byte into the register and the rest
abandoned, with no transmit interrupt to come back for them.  408 bytes
is what fits before that changeover.

And uartintr() returned without reading DR when RXNE was clear.  On
this part RXNEIE raises the interrupt for an overrun too, and ORE
clears only by reading SR and then DR, so a handler that checks RXNE,
finds it clear and returns has cleared nothing and is re-entered for
ever.  That one is latent -- it was not what stopped the console -- but
it is provoked by anything sending while nothing reads.

Neither shows under qemu.  The first needs a console that has been
enabled as one, the second needs bytes arriving unread.

What could not be found from software
-------------------------------------

The click was seated the wrong way round.  A mikroBUS click reversed
lines its pins up against the opposite header, so its receive and
transmit land on power, ground or an analogue input, and everything
measured through it agrees with everything else and is about a
connection that does not exist: silence on the six UART pins, silence
on every pin of every port driven by hand at every rate from 300 to
921600, and a receive LED that never flickered.  Reseating it was the
fix, and no instrument here was going to say so.

Two of the instruments were wrong in their own right, which is worth
remembering before trusting the next one.  A pin hunt that made every
pin an input and reported the first one to change named PA9, because an
input with no pull-up floats and floating CMOS toggles by itself; it
would have said the same with nothing attached.  Rebuilt with pull-ups
and a transition count, it then reported nothing, because three of the
five GPIO ports had no clock and read zero for ever.  Both looked like
findings.

The LEDs are what carried the bring-up.  LD1 lit as the first thing
archreset() does and LD2 blinking from the scheduler are the difference
between a board that is not running and a board that is running and
cannot be heard, and for hours that was the only fixed point there was.

The linker's -H4 output is a raw image whose first two words are the
initial stack pointer and the reset vector -- 0x200074fc and
0x080001c1, which is why qemu's -kernel takes it -- so it is also what
a ROM bootloader or a probe wants written at 0x08000000, with no
conversion.  There is no semihosting console in this tree, so a serial
line is not optional whichever way the image gets there: without one a
booted kernel has nowhere to say anything.

On a bare F407 the part's own ROM bootloader is the shortest way in.
It speaks a serial protocol on USART1 -- PA9 and PA10 -- entered by
holding BOOT0 high over a reset, so two wires and a USB serial adapter
both write the kernel and carry its shell:

	# BOOT0 high, then reset
	stm32flash -w istm32f407 -v -g 0x08000000 /dev/cu.SERIALPORT
	# BOOT0 back to ground, then reset
	screen /dev/cu.SERIALPORT 115200

On a clicker 2 that does not work, and the reason is in the next
section.

A probe is the other way, and the one to hand does not reach this
part.  The board is on the end of an Olimex ARM-USB-TINY-H, and openocd
reaches a DAP -- JTAG tap 0x4ba00477, and the debug and system power-up
handshakes in DP CTRL/STAT both acknowledged, 0xf0000001 -- but the
only access port on it reads IDR 0x24770002, which is an APB-AP.  An
STM32F4 answers 0x24770011, an AHB-AP, at the same index, and openocd
cannot read 0xe000ed00 through what is there.  Whatever is on the end
of that cable is not answering as an STM32F4.  With a probe that does:

	openocd -f interface/ftdi/olimex-arm-usb-tiny-h.cfg \
		-f target/stm32f4x.cfg \
		-c "program istm32f407 0x08000000 verify reset exit"

The board
---------

A MikroElektronika clicker 2 for STM32: an STM32F407VGT6, two mikroBUS
sockets, a USB Mini-B, two buttons and two LEDs.  From its manual
(clicker2-stm32-manual-v100.pdf) and the schematic in it, which is
where every pin below comes from:

	                  mikroBUS 1        mikroBUS 2
	AN                PA2               PA3
	RST               PE7               PE13
	CS                PE8               PE11
	SCK               PC10  (SPI3)      PB13  (SPI2)
	MISO              PC11  (SPI3)      PB14  (SPI2)
	MOSI              PC12  (SPI3)      PB15  (SPI2)
	PWM               PE9               PD12
	INT               PE10              PE14
	RX                PD6   (USART2)    PD9   (USART3)
	TX                PD5   (USART2)    PD8   (USART3)
	SCL               PA8   (I2C3)      PB10  (I2C2)
	SDA               PC9   (I2C3)      PB11  (I2C2)

	LD1 PE12   LD2 PE15   button T2 PE0   button T3 PA10

That table is why CONSUSART is CONSUSART2 rather than CONSUSART1.  PA9
and PA10 are not brought out on this board at all -- PA10 is button T3
and PA9 is the USB VBUS sense -- so the ROM bootloader's USART1 pins do
not exist here.  Its other pair, USART3 on PB10 and PB11, is mikroBUS
2's SCL and SDA and is reachable, and so is PC10/PC11 on mikroBUS 1's
SCK and MISO; but BOOT0 is on none of the headers or the 2x26 pad, so
the ROM bootloader cannot be entered without a wire to the part.

What the manual does say is that the board ships with a USB HID
bootloader already in its flash, reached by pressing Reset and
connecting within five seconds, and that the two remaining ways in --
mikroProg and ST-Link V2 -- both go through CN3, of which it says "2x5
headers should be first soldered on the CN3 connector pads".  So on a
stock board the programming header is not fitted, and soldering it is
what opens the probe paths.

The bootloader path needs no soldering and no probe, and it does not
need Windows either.  mikroe-uhb (github.com/thotypous/mikroe-uhb, MIT)
is a client for the same USB HID protocol, reverse engineered from
captures of the original, and it handles STM32F4XX.  It wants Intel
hex, which is what i$CONF.hex beside the kernel is:

	mikroe-uhb -v istm32f407.hex		# then press Reset

It is written for Python 2 -- implicit relative imports, xrange,
iteritems, dict.itervalues, a bare tuple after "in", and
file.xreadlines in the hex reader.  Its own test suite passes once
those are fixed: 4 of 4 in tests.device, including STM32Program, which
replays a capture of a real STM32 devkit being programmed and checks
the client emits the same USB traffic byte for byte; and every STM32
case in tests.devkit and tests.bootinfo.  The one that does not is
RandomBootInfo, which shells out to gcc -m32 to generate structs.

What that client says about the flash layout answers the question the
manual does not.  STM32DevKit lays the F4 out as 4 blocks of 16K, one
of 64K and 6 of 128K and asserts the total equals BootStart, so on a
1M part the bootloader is the last 128K sector, 0x080e0000 upward, and
the application begins at 0x08000000 -- where this kernel is already
linked.  Nothing has to move.  Loading istm32f407.hex into that model
fills 10 blocks up to 0xa0000, a quarter of a megabyte below the
bootloader.

It does change two things in the image, and both are expected.  The
reset vector at offset 4 becomes BootStart|1, so the part starts in
the bootloader and waits its five seconds; and a twenty-byte Thumb stub
is written just below BootStart which restores what was there.  Run on
this kernel, the first two words go from

	fc740020 c1010008    ->    fc740020 01000e00

and the stub assembles to movw/movt r0, #0x200074fc; mov sp, r0;
movw/movt r0, #0x080001c1; bx r0 -- this kernel's own initial stack
pointer and its own reset vector, thumb bit and all.  The stack pointer
in word 0 is never touched.

CN3 is full JTAG and not only SWD, which matters because it means an
ordinary 20-pin ARM JTAG probe drives it with jumper wires and no
adapter board.  Figure 3-13 of the manual gives the odd column as
VCC-3.3V, grounds and TRST, and the even column as

	2  TMS       4  TCK       6  TDO       8  TDI      10  RESET#

which is the ARM 20-pin signal set less the ground returns.

A ccRF 2 or ccRF 3 click carries the CC1120 and uses AN for its GPIO0,
RST for its reset, CS/SCK/MISO/MOSI for SPI, PWM for GPIO2 and INT for
GPIO3.  In mikroBUS 2 that is GPIO0 PA3, reset PE13, CS PE11, SPI2 on
PB13/PB14/PB15, GPIO2 PD12, GPIO3 PE14 -- which is the wiring
spistm32f4.c fills a Spidev in with.

What is here
------------

USART1 is on APB2 and USART2 and USART3 on APB1, which is not a
detail: the baud
divisor comes from whichever, and hsionly() in archstm32f407.c puts
both APB dividers back to one when the PLL does not lock.  Without
that, a fallback left HCLK at 16MHz with APB1 divided by four and APB2
by two -- because CFGR has to be written before the PLL is known to
have locked -- and the console would have come up at a fraction of the
baud it meant.

archstm32f407.c takes the clock tree as far as the part will let it and
no further.  setclock() tries HSI, then the PLL, and waits for each
ready bit with a bounded spin rather than forever, because qemu's
hw/misc/stm32_rcc.c is a plain register file that never sets HSIRDY or
PLLRDY: a kernel that waited for them would hang before its first
print.  It falls back to the 16MHz internal oscillator and sets apb1hz
to whatever it settled on, and uartstm32f4.c takes its divisor from
that, so a board whose PLL does not start has a console at the right
baud rather than at a tenth of it.  On silicon that fallback has never
been exercised either way.

uartstm32f4.c is a second file rather than an argument to
nucleof303/uartstm32.c because the F3 and the F4 share a name and a
manufacturer and not a register map: SR and DR here against the F3's
ISR, RDR and TDR, the enable bit at 13 rather than 0, and an overrun
cleared by reading SR and then DR rather than through an ICR this part
does not have.

UART0IRQ is the NVIC interrupt number -- 37 for USART1, 38 for USART2,
39 for USART3 -- and not that minus 16.  os/inferno/
armv7m/trap.c dispatches on IPSR minus Excirq and intrenable() uses its
argument as an NVIC ISER bit, so both want the number RM0090 calls
position 37, 38 or 39.  With the -16 the port transmitted and never
received a character.  nucleof303/mem.h had the same -16 and now has
38, which is its USART2; that
board has no userland to type at, so the correction there is by reading
and not by measurement.

I2C
---

i2cstm32f4.c registers both controllers a clicker 2 brings out -- I2C2
on mikroBUS 2's SCL and SDA, I2C3 on mikroBUS 1's -- and #J
(os/inferno/port/devi2c.c, the Osprey bus driver with its table sizes
made board tunables) serves them as /dev/i2c2 and /dev/i2c3.  Nothing
is clocked and no pin is driven until something opens #J, so a board
with nothing in a socket never touches the wires.

What has been run, and where:

Under qemu, with tools/qemu-patches/0046 and a ds1338 fitted to I2C2
(-device ds1338,bus=i2c2,address=0x68), on a booted kernel:

	; echo subaddress 1 >/dev/i2c2/i2c.68.ctl
	; bind /dev/i2c2 /n
	; dd -if /dev/user -of /n/i2c.68.data -bs 1 -seek 8
	7+0 records in
	7+0 records out
	; read -o 8 1 /n/i2c.68.data
	i
	; read -o 9 2 /n/i2c.68.data
	nf
	; read -o 11 3 /n/i2c.68.data
	ern

The probe finds 0x68 on i2c2 and nothing on i2c3, which is the truth
of that machine.  Seven bytes go into the part's NVRAM through
combined write transfers and come back through combined reads with a
repeated START, one, two and three at a time -- which is each of
RM0090's three master-receiver procedures in turn, the one-byte and
two-byte special cases included.

On silicon: nothing.  The two clicks to hand are a CC1120 radio and a
USB UART, neither of which is an I2C part, so the board half has never
addressed anything.  What is on the board is the emulated half's
opposite number and no more tested than that makes it.

Two things this turned up that are not I2C:

The portable devi2c.c has a 1024-entry device table and a 16-entry bus
table, 4160 bytes of bss.  On a part with 167936 bytes of pool that is
enough to turn a working shell into one that cannot fork: the first
"echo something >file" after boot wants a 4096-byte kernel stack for
the redirection's process and the arena has no room for it.  mem.h
sets NI2CDEV to 8 and NI2CBUS to 2 and the board has its memory back.
This is the third portable table this board has had to size for
itself, after NMNTTAG and STAGESIZE.

The ceiling check on the image size was written in rc in the mkfile
and this host builds with sh, so it had never run -- it failed with
"unexpected EOF while looking for matching `" and mk carried on.  It
is now an argument to tools/bin2ihex, which is C and runs under either
shell.

The analogue input
------------------

adcstm32f4.c converts the two mikroBUS AN pins -- PA2 and PA3, which
are ADC123_IN2 and IN3 -- and the part's own three internal channels:
the temperature sensor on IN16, the internal reference on IN17 and the
backup battery through its divider on IN18.  One read of /dev/adc
converts all five in one pass and gives a line each:

	; cat /dev/adc
	an1 263 211
	an2 398 320
	temp 2069 1667 387.8
	vref 2204 1776
	vbat 2339 1884 7536

Raw count first, because that is what the part said; then millivolts
at VREF+ = 3.3V, which is what a clicker 2 ties it to; then, for the
two channels where more can be said, degrees and the battery's own
millivolts.

(Those are the board's own numbers; what qemu prints is below.)

On silicon, and this is the first thing on this board that produced a
physical measurement rather than a register read:

	temp 944 760 25.0	the die, at room temperature
	vref 1516 1221		the 1.21V reference, so VREF+ is 3.27V
	vbat 2045 1647 3294	the 3.3V rail, through the divider
	an1 438 352		mikroBUS 1's AN, floating: an empty socket
	an2 0 0			mikroBUS 2's AN, held low by the CC1120

vref is the one that validates the rest.  1221mV read against a
datasheet typical of 1.21V says VREF+ is 3.27V, within a percent of
the 3.3V this file assumes, so every other millivolt figure is good to
about that.  And the temperature landing on 25.0 with uncalibrated
constants is luck as much as anything, but it is the right order.

vbat is where the board corrected the driver.  It first read 6588,
which is not a voltage this board has: an F405 or an F407 halves VBAT
before the converter, and it is an F427 or an F429 that quarters it.
With VBAT tied to the 3.3V rail the pin reads 1647mV, which is half.
The file said four, and no emulator could have caught it, because
under qemu the count is synthetic and four times a synthetic number is
just as synthetic.

an2 reading exactly zero rather than floating is itself information:
mikroBUS 2's AN is the click's GPIO0, and a CC1120 sitting in that
socket drives it.  An analogue input on a socket with a part in it is
an output belonging to something else.

Those other numbers are from qemu and none of them is a voltage.  What they
do show is the thing software can get wrong: 263 is channel 2 in the
top bits with a counter of 7 in the rest, 398 is channel 3 with 14,
and so on up to channel 18 with 35 -- so each conversion went to the
channel it was asked for and each was a fresh conversion.  A second
read gives the next five counts.  See tools/qemu-patches/0047 for why
the model says that and not a voltage, and why it now raises EOC at
all.

The temperature is the datasheet's typical V25 of 760mV and slope of
2.5mV per degree.  An F407 carries no factory calibration for it in
flash, unlike an F0 or an L4, so on silicon that reading is worth a
degree or two and not better; VREFINT against its 1.21V typical is the
honest way to get the true VREF+ and is in the file for that.

On silicon: not run.  #P is bound into /dev by os/inferno/init/
armv7minit.b, which this board already did for the arch files it did
not have until now.

The radio, on silicon: not there
--------------------------------

This board's first boot with the radio in it serves an empty #W:

	; ls '#W'
	; echo scan >/dev/radio0/ctl
	sh: cannot open /dev/radio0/ctl: file does not exist

which means cc1120init() read a PARTNUMBER that was not 0x48 and
declined to register a radio, exactly as it is meant to.  Everything
demonstrated for the CC1120 so far -- the packet path, the AX.25 UI
frame, the band sweep -- was between two qemu kernels.  The part in
mikroBUS 2 has never answered.

So #P/spi2 exists to ask again without a reboot:

	; cat /dev/spi2
	status 0x00 partnumber 0x00 partversion 0x00 miso 0

and "echo reset >/dev/spi2" pulses RESET_N and leaves the part to
start.  Under qemu with -machine netduinoplus2,radio=on the same file
reads

	status 0x10 partnumber 0x48 partversion 0x21 miso 0

so the file is known to report a part when there is one to report.

The board answered:

	; cat /dev/spi2
	status 0x0f partnumber 0x48 partversion 0x21 miso 1

A CC1120, awake -- CHIP_RDYn clear -- and idle.  The part was there
all along and the bus was reaching it; what was wrong was when
cc1120init() asked.  It strobed SRES and read PARTNUMBER immediately
after, and CHIP_RDYn stays high until the crystal is running, which is
milliseconds on a real part and instant in qemu's model.  So the
driver read 0x00 on every boot of the board and 0x48 on every boot of
the emulator, and had done since it was written.

cc1120.c now polls SNOP for CHIP_RDYn after the reset before reading
anything -- which is the handshake SWRU295E describes as watching SO
go low after CSn, in the form a Spidev can express, since it has no
way to read the MISO pin.  This is the one kind of fault an emulator
cannot have: a model that is ready the instant it is reset cannot fail
to be ready.

The RSSI the board first reported
---------------------------------

With the CC1120 finally registered, its stats read

	part CC1120 0x48 rev 0x21
	state 0x01
	rssi -6 last 0

-6dBm on 868MHz, in a room, on a click with no antenna, would be a
transmitter in the same hand.  It is the uncalibrated reading:
RSSI[11:0] out of reset is the level before the front end's gain has
been taken off it.  SWRU295E 6.10 says to add a calibrated offset,
which is found by putting a signal of known strength into the antenna
with AGC_GAIN_ADJUST at zero, and gives the worked example -102 --
the figure every TI example for this part uses.  -6 with that offset
is -108dBm, which is where a quiet 868MHz band belongs.

cc1120init() now writes -102 into AGC_GAIN_ADJUST so the register
reads dBm directly, and the stats carry the offset beside the reading:

	rssi -108 offset -102 last 0

That -102 is not a measurement of this board and the file says so in
both places.  "echo rssioffset <dB> >/dev/radio0/ctl" changes it, for
when there is a signal generator to calibrate against.

The stats also say "invalid" when RSSI_VALID is clear, which is what
qemu always shows, because the model has no RSSI at all.

What the radio cost in memory
-----------------------------

A registered radio is the difference between a board that can fork and
one that cannot.  At the 50/49/1 pool split this board had before,
with a CC1120 answering:

	; echo rssioffset -90 >/dev/radio0/ctl
	arena too large: main size 4096 cursize 74816 arenasize 80128
		maxsize 81900, alloc = 4288
	no procs for kproc

The 4288 is a new process's kernel stack: the shell forks for a ">"
redirection, and every ctl file on this board is written that way.
The split is 53/46/1 now and the conf beside this file has the
measurements.  ls(1) does not run with a radio registered at either
split, so nothing that worked was given up.

The other way out of that was a smaller kernel stack -- the 4096 is
what the 4288 is asking for -- and it was measured and rejected.
KSTACKWATCH reported a high water of 2476 across everything this board
had done, which makes 3072 look comfortable; with the radio actually
registered and taking interrupts the same watch reported 2548.  A
figure that moves by 72 bytes when a driver starts working is not a
figure to leave 524 bytes of headroom on, and a kernel stack that
overflows does not report, it corrupts whatever is under it.  The pool
split fails safely and a stack does not, so the pool split is what
moved.

The first band sweep off an antenna
-----------------------------------

61 steps, 868 to 928MHz, every one valid -- the RSSI_VALID wait in the
sweep does what it was written to do on a real part, where under qemu
it could only ever time out:

	; echo scan >/dev/radio0/ctl
	scan 868000000 -101
	scan 869000000 -108
	...
	scan 928000000 -107

Minimum -123, maximum -101, median -110, mean -109.9dBm.  The five
highest steps are 868.0 (-101), 887.0 (-102), 882.0 and 924.0 (-103)
and 909.0 (-104).

What that is and is not.  It is a real measurement of what reaches
this click's antenna port, taken by the part's own receiver with the
manufacturer's reference RSSI offset applied, and a floor near -110 is
what an unmatched CC1120 in a room should read.  It is not a spectrum
survey: each step is one sample taken the moment RSSI_VALID first
asserts, not an average, so the 22dB spread between the quietest and
loudest steps is sample variance as much as it is the band.  868.000
being the highest reading is suggestive -- the European SRD band that
meters and LoRa gateways sit in starts there -- and one sample at one
instant is not evidence of it.  Running the sweep twice and comparing
is what separates a feature of the band from a feature of the sample,
and costs nothing but the command.

The sweep also found a fault in this driver rather than in the band:
not one of the 61 lines said "carrier", and none of them could have.
AGC_CS_THR resets to 0x00, and with the RSSI offset programmed that
is a carrier sense threshold of 0dBm.  The stats and every sweep line
were reporting a condition that cannot occur, which reads like an
answer.  cc1120init() now sets it to -90dBm, twenty decibels above the
floor this sweep measured, and "echo csthr <dBm> >/dev/radio0/ctl"
changes it.  The stats print it, so the threshold a "carrier" was or
was not measured against is never invisible.

What two sweeps said, and what the sweep does now
-------------------------------------------------

The band was swept twice from the same place, minutes apart.  The
second run: minimum -121, maximum -105, median -111, mean -112.3.
Against the first (-123 to -101, median -110, mean -109.9) that looks
like the same band.  It is not the same band; it is the same noise.

	Pearson r between the two sweeps, step for step: -0.062
	Per-step difference: mean -2.4dB, sd 6.1, range -13 to +13

An r of zero means no step's reading in one sweep predicts anything
about the same step in the other.  Every feature in either sweep --
the -123 at 913MHz, the -101 at 868MHz, the twenty decibels between
the quietest and loudest steps -- was the variance of a single sample
of thermal noise.  868.000 happened to read highest in both, which is
where the European SRD band starts and is the one detail that
survives, but with 61 steps that coincidence is a one in sixty event
and not a detection.

There is nothing above about -105dBm to be heard from this bench, and
a single instantaneous sample per step could not have found it if
there were: anything that transmits in bursts is absent from most of
any second.

So the sweep dwells now.  Each step waits for the estimate to go
valid, then samples for a hundred milliseconds -- about a hundred
readings -- and reports the peak, the mean, the floor and how many
samples they are of, with "carrier" if carrier sense asserted at any
point during the dwell:

	scan 868000000 -108 -110 -112 100 4

six fields and then the word "carrier" or nothing, so awk(1) can have
it:

	awk '$1=="scan" && $7=="carrier" {print $2, $3}'

frequency, peak, mean, floor, sample count, and which sample the peak
was.  That last one is there to answer the question a peak alone
cannot -- whether a loud sample was something on the air or the sweep
hearing its own retune, which is always at the beginning of a dwell
while a signal is scattered through it

Sixty-one steps is about seven seconds.  "scan lo hi step ms" sets
the dwell, though the console will not carry a line that long.  The
part's own moving average is lengthened as well: AGC_CFG0's
RSSI_VALID_CNT goes from the reset 2 internal estimates to 9, which
is the maximum and is a quieter number for the same signal.

Checked on the emulator with tools/qemu-patches/0048, which gives the
model an RSSI it never had: the peak, mean and floor come out as
three different numbers, and with the threshold moved below the
modelled floor the stats say "carrier" -- the whole path from the ctl
file through AGC_CS_THR to RSSI0 and back.

What the dwelling sweep found, and what it was not
--------------------------------------------------

The first sweep with a hundred-millisecond dwell:

	scan 868000000 -110 -114 -118 98
	...
	scan 872000000  -84 -114 -118 98 carrier
	...

Flat mean of -113 to -114dBm on every one of 61 steps and a floor at
-118 -- which is what averaging a hundred samples is supposed to look
like, against the 20dB of scatter a single sample gave -- and on 15
of those steps a peak near -85, 28dB above the floor, each with
carrier sense asserted.  Scattered from 872 to 928 with no
clustering, which is not the shape of one transmitter.

Two explanations, and they are not distinguishable from a peak: a
real emitter that comes and goes, or this sweep hearing its own
retune, since setfreq() strobes SIDLE, SCAL and SRX and the first
valid estimate after the AGC restarts could be a settling artefact.
So the sweep says which sample the peak was.  A signal arrives when
it arrives; an artefact of tuning is always at the beginning.

The next sweep answered it, and the answer was not the one the
instrument was suspected of:

	scan 868000000 -106 -108 -113 98 16
	scan 869000000 -105 -109 -114 98 56
	...

No carrier anywhere, no peak above -104, and the peak indices
uniform from 0 to 86 -- the maximum of 98 samples of noise, landing
where chance puts it.  A retune artefact would have been at index 0
or 1 in every line of every sweep.  It was not there at all.

So the loud samples were external and intermittent: something was
transmitting across that band during one sweep and not during the
next.  That is as far as a sweep can take it, because a sweep spends
a tenth of a second on each frequency and then leaves.  "echo watch
>/dev/radio0/ctl" stays where the part is tuned and reports a line
every dwell for ten seconds, which is what says whether a frequency
is busy or was busy once.

Also from that: the part had never been tuned to a defined frequency.
cc1120init() left the FREQ registers at their reset value, which is
not a documented frequency, and the stats said "freq 0" because
nothing had called setfreq().  Two boards talked to each other only
because both were undefined in the same way.  It comes up on 868MHz
now.

One redirection, and how to transmit anyway
--------------------------------------------

This board can run exactly one "echo x >file" after boot.  The second
one hangs, in silence, whatever it is redirected to -- to the radio,
to a ctl file, anywhere.  It looks like a radio fault and is not one:
under qemu the first "echo hi >/dev/radio0/data" sends, "sent 1" in
the stats, the part back in RX with no timeout, and the second never
reaches the driver at all.

The shell spawns a process for a redirection and the process needs a
4096-byte kernel stack out of main, and main has not got a second one
to give.  Neither NPROC 8 nor a bigger KSTACK helps: 8 processes fail
in the same place, and KSTACK 8192 fails on the first redirection
rather than the second, because the stacks are what is eating the
pool.

It hangs rather than failing because os/inferno/port/alloc.c's
smalloc() retries for ever -- "for(;;) { poolalloc; tsleep(100) }" --
with no escape and nothing printed.  The comment above it already
records that as a known silent hang from an earlier hunt.  Making it
give up and raise Enomem was tried here and is worse: smalloc's
callers hold locks across it without a waserror() in sight, and the
board died with a stack pointer of 0x0ffffd90 -- 624 bytes below the
bottom of a kernel stack in CCM -- and "can't escalate 3 to
HardFault".  Left as it was, and written down.

What works, and is what to use:

	; cat >/dev/radio0/data
	hello1
	hello2
	hello3
	(control-D)

One open, one process, a packet per line, for as many as you like.
Five lines gives "sent 5" and the stats afterwards still read.  Any
file that takes a write can be driven this way, which is the way
round this board's ceiling until the ceiling moves.

Five packets sent, five TX FIFO underflows
-------------------------------------------

The board's answer after transmitting five lines through
"cat >/dev/radio0/data":

	sent 5 recv 0
	crcerr 0 toobig 0 txtimeout 0
	rxoverflow 0 txunderflow 5

Every transmit started and then ran out of data, which means the part
was told to send more bytes than it was given.  It was: PKT_CFG0 is
register 0x28 and this driver had it at 0x1c, which is AGC_CFG1.  So
the write that was meant to select variable packet length went into
the automatic gain control's configuration, PKT_CFG0 kept its reset
value of 0x00 -- fixed packet length -- and the part dutifully tried
to send PKT_LEN bytes, which is 125, for a packet of eight.

qemu reported five clean transmits for the same code, because its
model does not implement packet length modes: it sends whatever is in
the FIFO.  A model cannot catch a driver writing to the wrong
register when it does not implement the right one either, and this is
the second time in a day that a fault has been invisible in the
emulator and obvious on the board -- the first being cc1120init()
reading PARTNUMBER before the crystal was running.

Two constants beside it were also wrong and had done no harm:
RFEND_CFG1 and RFEND_CFG0 were 0x1e and 0x1f, which are FIFO_CFG and
DEV_ADDR, and nothing writes them.  Corrected rather than deleted,
since a wrong constant that is merely unused is a trap set for
whoever uses it next.

The stats now read PKT_CFG0 and PKT_LEN back out of the part rather
than reporting what the driver believes it wrote, and carry the
number of bytes staged in the TX FIFO before the last STX and the
part's own MARC_STATUS1 afterwards -- 0x40 for a transmit that
finished, 0x08 for one that underflowed.  Between them those three
numbers say whether a failed transmit was given too little, given
nothing at all, or asked for too much.

The register fix that was never applied
----------------------------------------

The PKT_CFG0 correction described above was committed and did not
happen.  The edit that was meant to change

	Rpktcfg0	= 0x1c,

matched nothing, because another constant sits between it and the two
lines the edit expected after it, and a replacement that matches
nothing changes nothing and says nothing.  Then it was "verified" by
reading the register back -- through the same wrong constant.  A
read-back proves the write and the read agree with each other.  It
says nothing whatever about which register they agree about.  0x1c is
AGC_CFG1, and "pktcfg0 0x20" in the stats was AGC_CFG1 handing back
what had just been put in it.

What the board was really saying, all along:

	txtest 4   var  staged   5  trace 1 0 0 5 4 7  left 0  marc 0x08
	txtest 40  var  staged  41  trace 1 0 0 5 4 7  left 0  marc 0x08
	txtest 100 var  staged 101  trace 1 0 0 5 4 2  left 9  marc 0x00
	txtest 4   fixed staged  4  trace 1 0 0 5 4 0  left 0  marc 0x40
	txtest 40  fixed staged 40  trace 1 0 0 5 4 0  left 0  marc 0x40

PKT_CFG0 was never written, so it held its reset value of 0x00, which
is fixed packet length, with PKT_LEN at 125.  Every "variable" packet
therefore asked the part for 125 bytes: five staged starved after
five, forty-one after forty-one, and a hundred and one was still
transmitting -- state 2, TX, nine bytes left -- when the five hundred
millisecond wait gave up on it, which is the txtimeout in the stats
and not a fault of the part's.

And "txmode fixed" worked because the part was already in fixed mode.
All that command really did was set PKT_LEN to the number of bytes
being sent, and the part then transmitted them and said so: marc 0x40,
"TX finished successfully", three times out of three.

So the transmitter has worked the whole time.  What was wrong was one
number in one enum, and then a check that could not see it.

Driving the console from the host
---------------------------------

tools/f407/console.py opens the board's UART and types at it, which is
worth having for the same reason tools/tinysa exists: an experiment
that needs a person to retype six commands is an experiment that gets
run once.

It enforces the two things this board cannot do, rather than letting
them be rediscovered.  A line longer than about fifty characters loses
its tail, so a longer one is refused instead of being sent and
silently truncated.  And the board spawns one process for a
redirection and no more, so a session that writes many lines to one
file opens it once:

	tools/f407/console.py ctl /dev/radio0/ctl 'reg 0x28' 'txtest 4'

The second "cat >file" of a boot wedges the board hard enough that
character echo stops -- which is worse than the silent hang already
recorded, since echo happens in the interrupt handler and survives a
blocked shell.  Whatever that is, it needs the reset button.

A bench that survives this board's one process
-----------------------------------------------

Writing a ctl file needs a redirection, a redirection needs a process,
and this board spawns one process in a boot.  So a bench driven
through ctl costs a reset for every session, and the second session of
a boot does not fail, it wedges the board hard enough to stop
character echo.

	; cat /dev/radio0/txtest
	txtest 125 var staged 126 trace 1 0 0 5 4 2 left 35 marc 0x00

A read costs nothing -- the shell runs cat in its own process -- so
the bench is a read now.  Each one sends a packet and reports what the
part did with it.  A read with a side effect is not something to do
lightly and this one is deliberate; it exists only on a driver that
offers it and the file is 0440.

What the board's registers say, read at the prompt with "reg":

	PA_CFG2      0x7f   power ramp 0x3f, which is +14dBm
	FS_CFG       0x02   820-960MHz band, LO divider 4
	FREQ2:0  6c 80 00   7110656, which is 868.000000MHz exactly
	SYMBOL_RATE  43 a9 2a   1200 baud

So the part is tuned where it was asked, at full power, and a 125 byte
packet at 1200 baud is most of a second on the air.  Six of those in a
row, with a tinySA sweeping 858 to 878MHz beside the board, produced
nothing above the -79dBm the room was already making.  The part says
it is in TX -- state 2, with bytes still to send -- and nothing comes
out that an instrument a hand's width away can hear.
